\description{
Compares two groups' association networks made by \code{\link[=assoc_network]{assoc_network()}}:
the differences in each feature's centralities and in the networks'
global properties, how much their most central features and hubs
overlap (Jaccard index), and how similar their clusterings are (adjusted
Rand index), as NetCoMi's \code{netCompare()} does. Given the count table
and the groups, it also tests the differences by permuting the samples'
groups.
}
\details{
\strong{Most central features.} Those above the \code{quantile} quantile of a
centrality over all the features (0 outside the largest connected
component), as for hubs in \code{\link[=assoc_network]{assoc_network()}}.

\strong{Jaccard index.} For two sets \eqn{A} and \eqn{B} of features, \eqn{J
= |A \cap B| / |A \cup B|} (0 when both are empty). \code{p_less} and
\code{p_greater} are \eqn{P(J \le j)} and \eqn{P(J \ge j)} under Real &
Vargas's (1996) model, as NetCoMi computes them: each of the \eqn{n =
|A \cup B|} features is in both sets with probability 1/3, so
\eqn{|A \cap B|} is Binomial(\eqn{n}, 1/3). (This depends only on the
union, not on how many features there are.)

\strong{Adjusted Rand index} (Hubert & Arabie 1985) of the two networks'
\code{cluster} columns as they are, so cluster 0 (features alone in their
cluster) is one cluster, as in NetCoMi. Its p-value tests the null
hypothesis ARI = 0: the cluster labels of \code{y} are permuted over the
features \code{permutations} times, and \eqn{p = (1 + \#\{|ARI_b| \ge
|ARI|\}) / (B + 1)}. This is two-sided, as NetCoMi's p-values are
(its documentation describes a one-sided test; its outputs, compared
over 20,000 permutations, are two-sided); NetCoMi adds no 1 to the
counts.

\strong{Group-permutation tests} (with \code{counts} and \code{groups}). Each
permutation shuffles the samples' groups, keeping the groups' sizes,
re-estimates both groups' associations with \code{\link[=feature_assoc]{feature_assoc()}} (with
\code{assoc_args}) and rebuilds both networks with \code{\link[=assoc_network]{assoc_network()}} (with
\code{network_args}). A difference's p-value is the proportion of the
permutations, counting the observed one, with an absolute difference at
least as large. This runs the association method \eqn{2B} times: with
\code{\link[=sparcc]{sparcc()}}, lower its \code{n_draws} in \code{assoc_args}, or \code{permutations}.
Permutation \eqn{b}'s association runs get their own \code{seed}, drawn from
(\code{seed}, \eqn{b}), replacing any \code{seed} in \code{assoc_args}.

\strong{Names in NetCoMi.} \code{quantile} is \code{jaccQuant}, \code{permutations} is
\code{nPermRand} and \code{nPerm} (with \code{permTest = TRUE}), and \code{p_adjust} is
\code{adjust}. NetCoMi's results \code{jaccDeg}, \code{jaccBetw}, \code{jaccClose},
\code{jaccEigen}, \code{jaccHub} and \code{randInd} are \code{jaccard} and \code{ari} here.

\strong{Differences from NetCoMi:} p-values are adjusted with
\code{\link[stats:p.adjust]{stats::p.adjust()}} only (NetCoMi's default is "adaptBH"); the ARI test's
p-value counts the observed value; the permutations come from ecodive's
own generator, so they match NetCoMi's in distribution only; no graphlet
distance; no comparison of the LCCs alone.
}
\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

\examples{
    # Two groups of 60 samples of 15 taxa
    set.seed(1)
    make <- function (linked) {
      basis <- matrix(rnorm(60 * 15), 60, 15)
      basis[, linked] <- basis[, linked] + basis[, linked[1]]
      matrix(rpois(900, exp(basis + 4)), 60, 15)
    }
    counts <- rbind(make(1:5), make(6:10))
    colnames(counts) <- paste0('OTU', 1:15)
    groups <- rep(c('a', 'b'), each = 60)

    nx <- assoc_network(sparcc(counts[groups == 'a', ], n_draws = 10))
    ny <- assoc_network(sparcc(counts[groups == 'b', ], n_draws = 10))
    cmp <- assoc_network_compare(nx, ny)
    cmp$jaccard
    cmp$ari
    plot(cmp)

    # Group-permutation tests (slow: 2 x 19 SparCC runs)
    cmp <- assoc_network_compare(
      nx, ny, permutations = 19, counts = counts, groups = groups,
      assoc_args = list(n_draws = 10) )
    cmp$global

}
